$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{x-1}{\sqrt{5x-1}-2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x-1}{\sqrt{5x-1}-2}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{5x-1}+2}{\sqrt{5x-1}+2}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the denominator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x-1)(\sqrt{5x-1}+2)}{(5x-1)-2^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x-1)}(\sqrt{5x-1}+2)}{5\cancel{(x-1)}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\sqrt{5x-1}+2}{5} \text{, for }x\neq 1
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $1$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
